>
  <h2>What is the Google profile and why should you care?</h2>
  <p>
    The Google profile (Google Business Profile) is your business’s listing on Google
    Search and Maps: name, hours, phone, photos, reviews and the
    “Directions” button so people can come find you. For most customers it’s their first
    impression of you — they see it before they even open your website.
  </p>
  <p>
    When someone searches “repair shop near me” or “pizzeria in the southern suburbs”, Google shows business
    profiles first, not websites. And AI tools like ChatGPT lean on the same
    details when you ask them to recommend a place. The profile is free — all it
    takes is setting it up right and keeping it alive.
  </p>

  <h2>How do you set it up, step by step?</h2>
  <p>
    You go to google.com/business, sign in with a Google account and follow the
    steps. You don’t need technical knowledge — you need a little patience and accurate details.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Claim or create.</strong> First search for your name on Maps. If the
      profile already exists (Google often creates it on its own), click “Claim this
      business”. If it doesn’t exist, create it from scratch.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Verification.</strong> Google wants proof that the business is yours —
      via a code in a letter, by phone or by video. Until that’s done, the profile doesn’t
      show properly.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Category.</strong> Pick the most specific one that fits you: “Pizzeria”,
      not “Restaurant”. That’s how Google understands which searches to show you in.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Hours.</strong> Enter your real opening hours — and remember to update them
      on holidays and vacations.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Photos.</strong> Upload at least 5 of your own, recent ones: the storefront,
      the interior, your products or your work. Not just a logo, and no photos off the internet.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Services and products.</strong> Write exactly what you offer, one by one. That way
      you also show up in more specific searches.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Description.</strong> Two or three simple sentences: what you do, where, and what
      sets you apart. Write the way you speak — no advertising slogans.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Why must your details be the same everywhere?</h2>
  <p>
    Because Google and the AI tools cross-check everything they find: your profile, your
    site, the directories, your social. When your name, address and phone match
    everywhere, they trust you; when every source says something different, they get confused —
    and so does the customer.
  </p>
  <p>
    Do a quick check: same name (written the same way), same address, same
    phone on your site, your Google profile, Facebook and Instagram, and in every
    directory you’re listed in. It’s one of the simplest things that count in SEO (how
    search ranks you) — we explain the bigger picture in the guide
    <a href="/en/guides/seo-in-plain-words/">SEO in plain words</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What are the most common mistakes?</h2>
  <p>
    Most profiles don’t suffer from anything complicated — they suffer from neglect. These
    are the ones you run into most often:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong hours.</strong> The profile says “Closed” while you’re open — or the
      other way round. The customer who showed up to a locked door rarely comes back.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Old photos, or none.</strong> Dark shots from another era, or only
      whatever customers happened to upload.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Unanswered reviews.</strong> Whoever reads the reviews also sees whether
      you reply — to the good ones and the bad ones.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong or too-generic category.</strong> Google doesn’t know where to show you,
      so often it doesn’t show you at all where it should.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Duplicate profiles.</strong> Two half-filled profiles split the reviews and
      confuse Maps. Better one, done right.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How does the profile tie into reviews?</h2>
  <p>
    The star next to your name on Maps comes from your Google reviews — and it’s
    one of the first things a customer looks at before choosing. A well-built profile without
    reviews stays half-finished.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two habits are enough to start: ask the happy customer to leave a couple of lines while
    the experience is fresh, and reply to every review within 1–2 days — politely, even
    when it’s unfair. How to set this up more systematically is explained in the guide
    <a href="/en/guides/more-google-reviews/">on getting more Google reviews</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How does PISMA help?</h2>
  <p>
    If you don’t want the hassle, we take it on: the Google Business Profile Setup costs
    {eur(gbpSetup.price)} one-off — claiming or creating, verification, category, hours,
    photos, services, description and details matching your site. You’ll find it on the
